  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The Vallon Sourn ("dark" in Provençal) is a small 3 km gorge carved by the Argens in the limestone plateau, between Châteauvert and Correns, in the Provence Verte. It is a protected natural site: walking, swimming, picnics, climbing and canoeing. From the car park, an 800 m walk leads to the banks and their beaches.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Note: the Provence Verte & Verdon tourist office reports the site as inaccessible, temporarily closed, following the summer 2026 wildfires (listing updated 14/09/2026). Check with the tourist office before you come.</p>
      <p>When the site is open, the tourist office asks that dogs be kept on a leash. Two wild beaches let you swim in the Argens; swimming is unsupervised and at users' own risk. The 43-space car park at Mas de Bagarèdes is free, with no parking from 9pm to 7am. The river's flow varies with the season.</p>
      <p>What the tourist office listing says for visiting with a dog: the valley is a Sensitive Natural Area of the Var Département (230 hectares, Natura 2000), parts of which, notably the south bank, are off-limits to protect the habitat; the cliffs reach 40 m and shade the Argens. From the car park, an 800 m walk leads to the banks; a disabled parking space in the heart of the valley gives access to the main beach by a boardwalk. On site: dry toilets at the foot of the cliffs and near the beach, picnic tables by the beach, no mobile signal, an emergency call point at the foot of the cliff (GPS 43.49232, 6.04371). Banned: hunting, fishing, fires, camping, picking plants, motor vehicles and, for recreational drone pilots, flying drones. From June to September, access to forest massifs is regulated by the prefecture (fire-risk map published every evening at 6pm) and the D45 is closed when the massifs are.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: before setting out, confirm the site has reopened with the tourist office (04 94 72 04 21).</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The villages of Correns and Châteauvert, as well as the gorges, are also popular for climbing (over 200 routes on the left bank) and canoeing.</p>
